WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.480
Super Bowl LVII was filled with memorable moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.720
and it was great to see interpreters for&nbsp;
the deaf and hearing impaired for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.500
time in the NFL. But did you know that you've&nbsp;
definitely seen one of the interpreters before?

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.820
The United States national anthem is part of&nbsp;
the opening of every Super Bowl. This year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.220
"The Star Spangled Banner"&nbsp;
was performed by eight-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.860
Grammy Award-winning country&nbsp;
music artist Chris Stapleton.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.340
This year is also the first year that the&nbsp;
Super Bowl has included interpretation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.640
the deaf for the national anthem, with&nbsp;
the National Football League bringing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.660
American Sign Language interpreters to relay&nbsp;
Francis Scott Key's well-known lyrics. Sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.140
language interpretation was also provided&nbsp;
for Babyface's performance of "America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.140
the Beautiful" and for Rihanna's&nbsp;
much-anticipated halftime show.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.760
Babyface's performance was interpreted by&nbsp;
University of Arizona researcher Colin Denny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.520
a member of the Navajo Nation who&nbsp;
signed "America the Beautiful"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.180
using a combination of A.S.L. and&nbsp;
North American Indian Sign Language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.680
which is still used by dozens of tribes to this&nbsp;
day. Meanwhile, African-American Philadelphia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.720
native and Bowie State University nursing student&nbsp;
Justina Miles provided interpretation for both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.040
Rihanna's performance and Sheryl Lee Ralph's&nbsp;
performance of "Lift Every Voice and Sing."

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.880
As for the fellow who provided sign language&nbsp;
for "The Star Spangled Banner," there's a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.900
chance you may recognize him from a variety&nbsp;
of movies and TV shows. If you're a fan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.160
"The Mandalorian," or "Scrubs," or just keep up&nbsp;
with recent Oscar winners, you'll recognize Troy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.260
Kotsur. Growing up in Mesa, Arizona, and Phoenix,&nbsp;
Kotsur was born deaf and studied film, theater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.400
and television at Gallaudet University. He&nbsp;
established a solid career in theater and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.340
has made appearances in several films&nbsp;
and television shows over the years.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:31.740
In 2019, Kotsur became the first deaf actor to act&nbsp;
in a "Star Wars" production, when he appeared as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:36.000
Tusken raider in an episode of "The Mandalorian,"&nbsp;
using a Tatooine version of sign language.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:40.800
"What are you doing?"

00:01:41.059 --> 00:01:42.259
"Negotiating."

00:01:42.259 --> 00:01:47.220
In 2022, he made history as the first deaf&nbsp;
male to be nominated for an Academy Award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.440
and, subsequently, to win an Academy Award.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.820
Kotsur won best supporting actor for his&nbsp;
performance in 2021's "CODA," in which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.300
played a deaf fisherman attempting to come to&nbsp;
terms with his hearing daughter's aspirations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.620
to become a singer. He appeared alongside&nbsp;
another history-maker, Marlee Matlin, who became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.240
the first deaf person to win an Oscar for her&nbsp;
performance in 1986's "Children of a Lesser God."

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.560
Despite only appearing in one episode, Troy&nbsp;
Kotsur's contributions to "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.160
are impressive — both as an actor and&nbsp;
behind-the-scenes maverick. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.160
he hasn't received the widespread&nbsp;
recognition he deserves due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.540
fact he took on the more unsung roles. His&nbsp;
face wasn't shown on camera and off-camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.440
work tends to be overlooked in general, but his&nbsp;
involvement helped bring a fun scene to life.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.100
The "Chapter 5: The Gunslinger" episode&nbsp;
of "The Mandalorian" sees our titular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.920
bounty hunter encounter a pair&nbsp;
of Tusken Raiders on Tatooine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.860
He must negotiate with them for&nbsp;
free passage across their land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.300
so he communicates in their native sign language&nbsp;
and gets the deal done. Kotsur plays one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.654
Tusken Raiders, but he's hidden behind layers&nbsp;
of prosthetics and unrecognizable as a result.

00:02:43.654 --> 00:02:47.640
Kotsur also helped the show's creators come up&nbsp;
with the Tusken sign language. The Oscar winner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.880
was initially brought in to help develop&nbsp;
a new form of sign language for the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.000
so he turned to "Star Wars" lore&nbsp;
for inspiration as opposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.960
using traditional American sign language.&nbsp;
For example, the sign for "Mandalorian"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.100
was based on the shape of the character's&nbsp;
helmet and the 'M' handshape. As viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.640
it's easy to overlook the smaller details&nbsp;
that go into bringing certain scenes to life;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.121
however, Kotsur's work actually enriched the&nbsp;
Tusken mythos and the "Star Wars" universe.

